# AIForge local environment — copy to `.env` (or `aiforge.env`) and edit.
#   cp .env.example .env
# `./run.sh` sources this automatically (set -a auto-exports every key).
# NEVER commit a real `.env` / `aiforge.env` — they are gitignored.
# Lines are `KEY=value`; no `export`, no quotes needed for simple values.

# ──────────────────────────── Model endpoint ─────────────────────────
# The OpenAI-compatible base_url AIForge points the model role(s) at.
# Self-hosted vLLM / LM Studio / Ollama / TGI / LiteLLM all work here.
# Use https:// for a TLS-fronted self-hosted box.
AIFORGE_LM_BASE_URL=https://your-self-hosted-box:1234/v1
# Optional API key for the endpoint (blank for keyless OSS endpoints).
# AIFORGE_LM_API_KEY=

# Per-role overrides (optional) — one served model per role/port:
#   AIFORGE_DOER_BASE_URL=https://your-box:1234/v1
#   AIFORGE_PLANNER_BASE_URL=https://your-box:1235/v1
#   AIFORGE_SUPERVISOR_BASE_URL=https://your-box:1236/v1
# Generic OpenAI-compat fallback for ALL roles:
#   AIFORGE_OPENAI_COMPAT_BASE_URL=https://your-box:1234/v1
#   AIFORGE_OPENAI_COMPAT_API_KEY=

# ── Headroom compression (optional) ──────────────────────────────────
# The `headroom` docker-compose service (services/headroom_proxy) is a local
# LLM-traffic compressor that shrinks accumulated TOOL OUTPUTS (grep results,
# file reads, test logs) before they hit the model.
#
# It runs as a transparent FORWARDING PROXY — the clean, one-place-to-manage
# design. Flip ONE knob and run.sh --hybrid brings up the sidecar AND the app
# repoints its local model endpoint at it (llm/headroom.proxy_base). Every
# agent — architect, planner, doer, refiner, verifier, chat, … — flows through
# it automatically, because both resolver boundaries (client.py and the ADK
# LiteLlm path) pass their base_url through the same redirect. No per-agent
# wiring, no per-call code:
#   AIFORGE_HEADROOM=1
# The proxy compresses, then forwards to the REAL model. ONE env sets both the
# forward target AND which endpoint the app redirects (they can't drift):
#   AIFORGE_HEADROOM_UPSTREAM=http://127.0.0.1:1234   (default: loopback LM Studio)
# Only THAT endpoint is routed through the proxy — cloud escalation targets and
# any other local box are left untouched. Advanced: move the proxy itself with
#   AIFORGE_HEADROOM_URL=http://host:8787             (default http://127.0.0.1:8787)
#
# Timeouts layer so the proxy is the MOST patient hop (it must never give up on
# the model before the app does) and the app is the one that fails+retries a
# genuinely wedged call. Defaults handle a big local chain-of-thought:
#   HEADROOM_REQUEST_TIMEOUT=1200   proxy→model read timeout (20 min)
#   AIFORGE_LLM_TIMEOUT_S=900       app→model read timeout (15 min) — keep < proxy
#   AIFORGE_CHAT_LLM_RETRIES=3      transient retries in chat before surfacing
#
# Kompress-v2 (the ML text crusher) is ON by default in the compose sidecar
# (docker-compose.yml → headroom service env). Without it the router only runs
# SmartCrusher, which crushes JSON-array tool outputs but leaves plain text
# (file reads, grep, test logs) at ~0%. Kompress compresses those ~29% and —
# because COMPRESS_USER_MESSAGES is on — the ReAct doer's role:user OBSERVATION
# turns too. It is LOSSY/semantic (worked in testing, all tests still passed),
# so disable per-deploy if a task ever needs the doer to reproduce an EXACT
# string from a tool output:
#   HEADROOM_FORCE_KOMPRESS_ALL=0        # SmartCrusher (JSON) only, no ML crush
#   HEADROOM_COMPRESS_USER_MESSAGES=0    # re-protect user/OBSERVATION messages
# Kompress-v2-base (ONNX, ~a few hundred MB) auto-downloads from HuggingFace to
# the hf-cache volume on first use; onnxruntime ships in the sidecar image.

# ─────────────────────────────── TLS / SSL ───────────────────────────
# Applies ONLY to AIForge's own self-hosted services (model endpoint,
# embed/rerank sidecars, memory/neo4j-http, MCP, local probes, AIForge
# API). Public hosts (GitHub API, arbitrary web/doc fetches) are ALWAYS
# verified regardless of these settings.
#
# PREFERRED — keep verification ON, trust your internal CA / self-signed
# cert by pointing at its PEM. Works for any https host.
# AIFORGE_LLM_CA_BUNDLE=/path/to/internal-ca.pem
#
# OR — relax verification entirely, but only for INTERNAL/LAN hosts
# (loopback, RFC-1918 private IPs, .local/.lan names, and the configured
# base_url hosts above). Default is `true` (verify). Set `false` only for
# a box you control whose self-signed cert you can't add to a CA bundle.
AIFORGE_LLM_SSL_VERIFY=true

# ─────────────────────── Self-hosted sidecars (optional) ─────────────
# Only set if you run these on non-default hosts/ports.
# AIFORGE_EMBED_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8764
# AIFORGE_RERANK_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8765
# AIFORGE_API_BASE=http://localhost:8799
# AIFORGE_MCP_ENDPOINTS=mongo=http://127.0.0.1:8810/mcp,k8s=http://127.0.0.1:8811/mcp

# ─────────────────────────── Confluence (chat) ───────────────────────
# Chat tools to search / read / create / update Confluence pages
# (Server / Data Center). Create a Personal Access Token in Confluence:
#   your avatar → Settings → Personal Access Tokens → Create token.
# Writes (create/update) go through the chat approval gate by default.
# CONFLUENCE_BASE_URL=https://confluence.yourcompany.internal
# CONFLUENCE_TOKEN=your-personal-access-token
# CONFLUENCE_USER=you@company.com        # set ⇒ Basic auth (user+token) instead of Bearer
# CONFLUENCE_INSECURE_TLS=1              # only for a self-signed internal cert

# ─────────────────────────── Jira (chat) ─────────────────────────────
# Chat tools to search / read / create / update / comment on Jira issues
# (Server / Data Center). Create a Personal Access Token in Jira:
#   your avatar → Profile → Personal Access Tokens → Create token.
# Writes (create/update/comment) go through the chat approval gate by default.
# JIRA_BASE_URL=https://jira.yourcompany.internal
# JIRA_TOKEN=your-personal-access-token
# JIRA_USER=you@company.com              # set ⇒ Basic auth (user+token) instead of Bearer
# JIRA_INSECURE_TLS=1                    # only for a self-signed internal cert

# ─────────────────────────── Email (chat) ───────────────────────────
# Chat/agent tools to SEND (SMTP) and READ (IMAP) email via YOUR OWN mail
# provider. Config-gated like Jira: unset host ⇒ the tool reports
# "unconfigured" and does nothing. All values optional. For Gmail/M365 use an
# app password, not your login password. Sends go through the approval gate.
# Master kill switch: AIFORGE_EMAIL_DISABLE=1 disables both tools.
# AIFORGE_SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
# AIFORGE_SMTP_PORT=587                   # 587 STARTTLS (default) · 465 for SSL
# AIFORGE_SMTP_USER=you@gmail.com
# AIFORGE_SMTP_PASSWORD=your-app-password
# AIFORGE_SMTP_FROM=you@gmail.com         # default = SMTP_USER
# AIFORGE_SMTP_STARTTLS=1                 # "0" ⇒ implicit SSL (port 465)
# AIFORGE_IMAP_HOST=imap.gmail.com
# AIFORGE_IMAP_PORT=993
# AIFORGE_IMAP_USER=you@gmail.com
# AIFORGE_IMAP_PASSWORD=your-app-password
# AIFORGE_IMAP_SSL=1                      # "0" ⇒ plain IMAP4 (rarely needed)
# AIFORGE_EMAIL_DISABLE=1                 # kill switch for both tools

# ─────────────────────────── GitLab (chat) ───────────────────────────
# Chat tools to search / read / create / update / comment on GitLab issues
# (self-managed or gitlab.com). Create a Personal/Project/Group Access
# Token: avatar → Edit profile → Access Tokens (read_api, + api for writes).
# Writes (create/update/comment) go through the chat approval gate by default.
# GITLAB_BASE_URL=https://gitlab.yourcompany.internal   # host root, NO /api/v4
# GITLAB_TOKEN=your-access-token
# GITLAB_PROJECT=group/subgroup/project  # optional default project (or numeric id)
# GITLAB_OAUTH=1                         # token is OAuth ⇒ sent as Bearer (default PRIVATE-TOKEN)
# GITLAB_INSECURE_TLS=1                  # only for a self-signed internal cert

# ─────────────────────────── Web search (chat) ───────────────────────
# Keyless web search + page fetch (DuckDuckGo) — the agent searches the
# open web when it's stuck. No API key. Read-only. Enabled by default.
# AIFORGE_WEB_SEARCH_DISABLE=1           # hard-off the web_search / web_fetch tools
# AIFORGE_WEB_SEARCH_RESULTS=5           # default result count
# AIFORGE_WEB_TIMEOUT_S=12               # per-request timeout (seconds)

# ─────────────────────────── Pipeline routing ────────────────────────
# Triage fast-path: a 'trivial' ticket skips enhancer→research→planner→
# verifiers and goes straight to the Doer. Set this to ALWAYS run the full
# pipeline (every agent executes + shows in the trace), regardless of triage.
# AIFORGE_FORCE_FULL_PIPELINE=1
#
# Per-subtask FRESH-CONTEXT execution (the scale path). Team mode: enhance →
# architect designs the file list → each file becomes a subtask that runs in its
# OWN fresh context + git worktree, so a big multi-file build NEVER exhausts one
# context. Planner writes SPEC.md (shared requirements) up front; every subtask
# builds against it; a final pass verifies the merged tree covers SPEC.md.
# Sequential on a local single-model endpoint (no false parallelism). STRONGLY
# recommended for large tasks (interpreters, multi-module packages, 5k+ LOC).
# AIFORGE_PARALLEL_SUBTASKS=1
# AIFORGE_PARALLEL_SUBTASKS_MAX=4   # concurrency on a batching/cloud endpoint
#
# Drop the parallel LLM context branches (researcher + ctx_repomap +
# ctx_conventions). They drive NATIVE function-calling, which on a local
# mlx/LM-Studio endpoint intermittently drops a tool_call/result pairing
# ("Missing tool results for tool_call_id") and — being on the join's critical
# path — aborts the WHOLE graph. They're pure enrichment (pre-flight
# memory_brief_md + aider repo_map already seed context), so on local/large
# runs skipping them makes the pipeline far more robust. RECOMMENDED for local
# providers building big multi-file tasks.
# AIFORGE_SKIP_CTX_BRANCHES=1
#
# Doer-loop iteration budget (dynamic; scales with plan size). The ceiling is
# max(tier, ITERS_PER_SUBTASK × #subtasks), clamped to _CAP. Raise for very
# large tasks (10k+ LOC). Defaults: MODERATE=20 COMPLEX=40 PER_SUBTASK=6 CAP=200.
# AIFORGE_MAX_DOER_ITERS_COMPLEX=60
# AIFORGE_MAX_DOER_ITERS_PER_SUBTASK=8
# AIFORGE_MAX_DOER_ITERS_CAP=300
#
# Bound a runaway/wedged LOCAL generation. A looping mlx/LM-Studio model can
# burn the full max_tokens (default 32768) or the full read timeout (default
# 900s) on ONE call, blocking the whole pipeline. On a large task that compounds
# into "runs forever". A tighter token cap shortens the runaway itself (16384
# still fits an ~800-line file per tool call). NOTE: only lower the read timeout
# below the default if your model is fast AND you don't route through the
# headroom proxy — with the proxy (compress+forward) a legit reasoning call
# needs the generous default, or it surfaces as "model didn't respond".
# AIFORGE_LLM_TIMEOUT_S=180      # only for a fast, no-proxy local endpoint
# AIFORGE_LLM_MAX_TOKENS=16384

# ─────────────────────────── App serving (chat/pipeline) ─────────────
# The `serve` tool runs an app in the background and returns its endpoint.
# A forgotten service auto-stops after this TTL (seconds); 0 disables.
# AIFORGE_SERVE_TTL_S=1800              # 30 min default

# ─────────────────────────── Host data + workspace (docker stack) ────
# ALL persistent data lives on the HOST under AIFORGE_DATA_DIR (git-ignored),
# not in docker named volumes — inspect/back it up straight from disk.
# AIFORGE_DATA_DIR=./data                 # postgres/, neo4j/, aiforge/, hf-cache/
# NEO4J_DATA_DIR=./data/neo4j             # override the graph data path
# The agent's repo workspace (AIFORGE_REPO_ROOT inside the container). Point
# it at your projects dir to have the agent work on your EXISTING local repos:
# AIFORGE_HOST_WORKSPACE=/Users/you/code  # default ./data/workspace
